1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,805
If you were a teenage girl in

2
00:00:01,805 --> 00:00:04,483
nineteen sixty-five, you were living in one

3
00:00:04,483 --> 00:00:06,974
of the most exciting moments in American

4
00:00:06,974 --> 00:00:08,219
history to be young.

5
00:00:08,399 --> 00:00:10,431
The Beatles were on the radio.

6
00:00:10,611 --> 00:00:12,283
Skirts were getting shorter.

7
00:00:12,463 --> 00:00:14,626
Your mother had grown up thinking her

8
00:00:14,626 --> 00:00:16,087
only future was marriage,

9
00:00:16,087 --> 00:00:18,074
but you were starting to wonder if

10
00:00:18,074 --> 00:00:19,243
there might be more.

11
00:00:19,423 --> 00:00:21,110
And the whole world seemed to be

12
00:00:21,110 --> 00:00:22,744
changing right in front of you.

13
00:00:23,094 --> 00:00:24,748
In nineteen sixty-five,

14
00:00:24,748 --> 00:00:27,698
there were more teenagers in America than

15
00:00:27,698 --> 00:00:28,562
ever before.

16
00:00:28,742 --> 00:00:31,296
The oldest baby boomers were turning nineteen.

17
00:00:31,476 --> 00:00:33,252
High schools were packed.

18
00:00:33,432 --> 00:00:35,970
Teen magazines, record companies,

19
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:39,354
clothing stores and cosmetic brands were all

20
00:00:39,354 --> 00:00:43,045
chasing teenage girls and their allowance money.

21
00:00:43,225 --> 00:00:44,392
For the first time,

22
00:00:44,392 --> 00:00:46,479
they were one of the most powerful

23
00:00:46,479 --> 00:00:48,566
groups of shoppers in the country.

24
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:51,262
So let's spend some time as a

25
00:00:51,262 --> 00:00:54,175
teenage girl in nineteen sixty-five.

26
00:00:54,355 --> 00:00:56,160
Her bedroom, her clothes,

27
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:57,749
her school, her music,

28
00:00:57,749 --> 00:01:00,277
her dates, her dreams and the rules

29
00:01:00,277 --> 00:01:01,216
she lived by.

30
00:01:01,396 --> 00:01:02,457
If that was you,

31
00:01:02,457 --> 00:01:03,452
or your sister,

32
00:01:03,452 --> 00:01:04,446
or your mother,

33
00:01:04,446 --> 00:01:06,899
I think you'll remember every detail.

34
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:08,963
Her bedroom was her kingdom,

35
00:01:08,963 --> 00:01:10,799
if she was lucky enough not to

36
00:01:10,799 --> 00:01:12,207
share it with a sister.

37
00:01:12,387 --> 00:01:14,930
A twin bed with a chenille bedspread.

38
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:17,211
A vanity table with a mirror.

39
00:01:17,391 --> 00:01:19,821
A record player and a stack of

40
00:01:19,821 --> 00:01:20,793
forty-fives.

41
00:01:20,973 --> 00:01:22,442
And on the walls,

42
00:01:22,442 --> 00:01:24,862
pictures cut from magazines.

43
00:01:25,042 --> 00:01:27,360
Paul McCartney, Herman's Hermits,

44
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,538
the Beach Boys and maybe Elvis,

45
00:01:29,538 --> 00:01:31,927
if she was a little old-fashioned.

46
00:01:32,277 --> 00:01:35,003
On her nightstand was a princess phone,

47
00:01:35,003 --> 00:01:37,310
if her parents had splurged for a

48
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:39,268
second line, or more likely,

49
00:01:39,268 --> 00:01:41,645
she stretched the long cord of the

50
00:01:41,645 --> 00:01:44,511
kitchen phone into the hallway closet for

51
00:01:44,511 --> 00:01:45,071
privacy.

52
00:01:45,251 --> 00:01:47,279
She talked to her best friend for

53
00:01:47,279 --> 00:01:49,922
hours, until her father yelled that someone

54
00:01:49,922 --> 00:01:51,950
else might need to use the phone.

55
00:01:52,299 --> 00:01:53,482
And in a drawer,

56
00:01:53,482 --> 00:01:55,553
she kept a diary with a tiny

57
00:01:55,553 --> 00:01:56,514
lock and key.

58
00:01:56,694 --> 00:01:58,493
In it, she wrote about the boy

59
00:01:58,493 --> 00:02:00,652
who sat behind her in history class,

60
00:02:00,652 --> 00:02:02,511
the fight with her best friend,

61
00:02:02,511 --> 00:02:04,310
and her dreams for the future.

62
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:07,468
Many women still have those diaries today,

63
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:08,673
and reading them,

64
00:02:08,673 --> 00:02:11,225
they say, is like meeting a stranger

65
00:02:11,225 --> 00:02:12,501
they used to know.

66
00:02:12,851 --> 00:02:15,738
Her reading came from the drugstore magazine

67
00:02:15,738 --> 00:02:16,066
rack.

68
00:02:16,246 --> 00:02:19,567
Seventeen magazine was her guide to fashion,

69
00:02:19,567 --> 00:02:21,227
dating and growing up.

70
00:02:21,407 --> 00:02:25,457
Tiger Beat, which launched in nineteen sixty-five,

71
00:02:25,457 --> 00:02:28,129
was full of pinups of teen idols.

72
00:02:28,309 --> 00:02:30,475
And she read Nancy Drew mysteries,

73
00:02:30,475 --> 00:02:31,685
Gone with the Wind,

74
00:02:31,685 --> 00:02:33,914
and maybe a paperback that she kept

75
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:36,398
hidden under the mattress so her mother

76
00:02:36,398 --> 00:02:37,481
wouldn't find it.

77
00:02:37,832 --> 00:02:39,817
Hair was a serious matter.

78
00:02:39,997 --> 00:02:41,396
In the early sixties,

79
00:02:41,396 --> 00:02:42,528
the look was big.

80
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:44,647
The bouffant and the beehive,

81
00:02:44,647 --> 00:02:46,920
teased high and held in place with

82
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:48,257
clouds of hairspray.

83
00:02:48,437 --> 00:02:50,581
Girls slept on giant rollers,

84
00:02:50,581 --> 00:02:53,390
sometimes made from orange juice cans,

85
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:55,903
to get their hair smooth and full.

86
00:02:56,083 --> 00:02:58,155
Some even ironed their hair on the

87
00:02:58,155 --> 00:03:00,227
ironing board to make it straight.

88
00:03:00,578 --> 00:03:02,422
By nineteen sixty-five,

89
00:03:02,422 --> 00:03:04,107
styles were changing.

90
00:03:04,287 --> 00:03:07,174
The Beatles and the British Invasion brought

91
00:03:07,174 --> 00:03:08,617
new looks from London.

92
00:03:08,797 --> 00:03:11,537
Long, straight hair with bangs was in.

93
00:03:11,717 --> 00:03:13,099
So was the short,

94
00:03:13,099 --> 00:03:16,594
sharp haircut made famous by Vidal Sassoon.

95
00:03:16,774 --> 00:03:19,280
Girls with naturally curly hair suffered,

96
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:21,603
and tried everything to straighten it.

97
00:03:21,954 --> 00:03:24,322
Makeup was usually a battle with Mom.

98
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:27,167
Many mothers didn't allow it until a

99
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:29,166
certain age, and even then,

100
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:30,202
only a little.

101
00:03:30,382 --> 00:03:32,043
Pale pink lipstick.

102
00:03:32,223 --> 00:03:33,638
Heavy black eyeliner,

103
00:03:33,638 --> 00:03:35,659
if she could get away with it.

104
00:03:35,839 --> 00:03:38,695
Fake eyelashes for special occasions.

105
00:03:38,875 --> 00:03:41,160
She bought it at the drugstore or

106
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:44,275
the five-and-dime with her babysitting money,

107
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:46,629
and sometimes put it on at school,

108
00:03:46,629 --> 00:03:48,291
after leaving the house.

109
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,521
Clothes were changing fast.

110
00:03:50,701 --> 00:03:54,102
At school, dress codes still required girls

111
00:03:54,102 --> 00:03:56,158
to wear skirts or dresses.

112
00:03:56,338 --> 00:03:57,636
No pants allowed,

113
00:03:57,636 --> 00:03:59,240
not even in the snow.

114
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,712
Skirts had to reach a certain length,

115
00:04:01,712 --> 00:04:02,950
and in some schools,

116
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:04,869
girls were made to kneel on the

117
00:04:04,869 --> 00:04:07,408
floor, so the teacher could check whether

118
00:04:07,408 --> 00:04:09,080
the hem touched the ground.

119
00:04:09,429 --> 00:04:11,554
But fashion was moving the other way.

120
00:04:11,734 --> 00:04:13,537
In nineteen sixty-five,

121
00:04:13,537 --> 00:04:16,201
the miniskirt arrived from London,

122
00:04:16,201 --> 00:04:19,257
made famous by the designer Mary Quant.

123
00:04:19,437 --> 00:04:22,061
Hemlines rose higher every season.

124
00:04:22,241 --> 00:04:23,611
Parents were horrified.

125
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:25,888
Girls rolled their skirts up at the

126
00:04:25,888 --> 00:04:27,746
waistband on the way to school,

127
00:04:27,746 --> 00:04:29,963
and rolled them back down before they

128
00:04:29,963 --> 00:04:30,502
got home.

129
00:04:30,851 --> 00:04:33,850
Other must-haves were the shift dress,

130
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:35,192
the A-line skirt,

131
00:04:35,192 --> 00:04:37,244
knee socks, penny loafers,

132
00:04:37,244 --> 00:04:38,664
Peter Pan collars,

133
00:04:38,664 --> 00:04:41,505
and a cardigan sweater with a little

134
00:04:41,505 --> 00:04:42,531
sweater clip.

135
00:04:42,711 --> 00:04:44,871
Go-go boots were the height of cool.

136
00:04:45,051 --> 00:04:46,259
And for the beach,

137
00:04:46,259 --> 00:04:47,937
a two-piece bathing suit,

138
00:04:47,937 --> 00:04:49,683
if her parents allowed it.

139
00:04:49,863 --> 00:04:50,850
Many didn't.

140
00:04:51,199 --> 00:04:54,021
Many girls sewed their own clothes.

141
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:57,135
Home economics class taught every girl to

142
00:04:57,135 --> 00:04:58,638
use a sewing machine,

143
00:04:58,638 --> 00:05:01,715
and she bought patterns from Simplicity and

144
00:05:01,715 --> 00:05:03,791
McCall's at the fabric store.

145
00:05:03,971 --> 00:05:06,431
A teenage girl could make a new

146
00:05:06,431 --> 00:05:08,337
dress for a few dollars,

147
00:05:08,337 --> 00:05:09,369
and many did,

148
00:05:09,369 --> 00:05:12,544
copying the styles they saw in Seventeen

149
00:05:12,544 --> 00:05:13,259
magazine.

150
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:15,080
And she earned money for all of

151
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:15,222
it.

152
00:05:15,402 --> 00:05:17,834
Babysitting was the classic job,

153
00:05:17,834 --> 00:05:20,418
paying around fifty cents an hour.

154
00:05:20,598 --> 00:05:23,570
Some girls worked at the five-and-dime,

155
00:05:23,570 --> 00:05:25,705
the drugstore soda fountain,

156
00:05:25,705 --> 00:05:28,144
the movie theater or as a carhop

157
00:05:28,144 --> 00:05:29,363
at the drive-in.

158
00:05:29,543 --> 00:05:31,598
In summer, they picked fruit,

159
00:05:31,598 --> 00:05:34,290
worked at the swimming pool snack bar,

160
00:05:34,290 --> 00:05:36,486
or helped at a family business.

161
00:05:36,836 --> 00:05:38,404
Music was everything.

162
00:05:38,584 --> 00:05:40,292
In nineteen sixty-five,

163
00:05:40,292 --> 00:05:43,412
a teenage girl's radio played the Beatles'

164
00:05:43,412 --> 00:05:44,823
Help and Yesterday,

165
00:05:44,823 --> 00:05:46,235
the Supremes' Stop!

166
00:05:46,415 --> 00:05:47,878
In the Name of Love,

167
00:05:47,878 --> 00:05:50,292
the Rolling Stones' Satisfaction,

168
00:05:50,292 --> 00:05:52,633
Sonny and Cher's I Got You Babe,

169
00:05:52,633 --> 00:05:53,950
and the Byrds' Mr.

170
00:05:54,130 --> 00:05:55,430
Tambourine Man.

171
00:05:55,610 --> 00:05:59,558
Motown, the British Invasion and folk rock,

172
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:00,660
all at once.

173
00:06:01,010 --> 00:06:03,396
In August of nineteen sixty-five,

174
00:06:03,396 --> 00:06:06,144
the Beatles played Shea Stadium in New

175
00:06:06,144 --> 00:06:08,891
York, in front of more than fifty-five

176
00:06:08,891 --> 00:06:09,904
thousand fans.

177
00:06:10,084 --> 00:06:12,442
It was the biggest concert in history

178
00:06:12,442 --> 00:06:13,462
up to that time.

179
00:06:13,642 --> 00:06:15,850
The screaming was so loud that the

180
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:18,124
band couldn't hear themselves play.

181
00:06:18,304 --> 00:06:21,049
Most of the screamers were teenage girls,

182
00:06:21,049 --> 00:06:22,992
and many of them still say it

183
00:06:22,992 --> 00:06:25,537
was the greatest night of their lives.

184
00:06:25,886 --> 00:06:29,195
At home, she watched American Bandstand,

185
00:06:29,195 --> 00:06:31,097
Shindig and Hullabaloo,

186
00:06:31,097 --> 00:06:34,488
where young dancers in go-go boots danced

187
00:06:34,488 --> 00:06:35,232
in cages.

188
00:06:35,412 --> 00:06:37,270
She learned the newest dances.

189
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:39,365
The Frug, the Watusi,

190
00:06:39,365 --> 00:06:42,466
the Swim, the Jerk and the Monkey.

191
00:06:42,646 --> 00:06:44,414
And she practiced in front of the

192
00:06:44,414 --> 00:06:46,451
mirror with her best friend until they

193
00:06:46,451 --> 00:06:47,255
had it perfect.

194
00:06:47,604 --> 00:06:48,991
And on Friday nights,

195
00:06:48,991 --> 00:06:50,774
there were slumber parties.

196
00:06:50,954 --> 00:06:52,676
Six girls in pajamas,

197
00:06:52,676 --> 00:06:55,547
in sleeping bags on the living room

198
00:06:55,547 --> 00:06:56,039
floor.

199
00:06:56,219 --> 00:06:58,913
Pizza, potato chips and soda.

200
00:06:59,093 --> 00:07:00,565
Prank calls to boys,

201
00:07:00,565 --> 00:07:02,332
giggling into the phone.

202
00:07:02,512 --> 00:07:05,751
Ouija boards and scary stories after midnight.

203
00:07:05,931 --> 00:07:08,643
Hair in rollers and faces covered in

204
00:07:08,643 --> 00:07:09,472
cold cream.

205
00:07:09,652 --> 00:07:11,707
And almost no sleep at all.

206
00:07:12,057 --> 00:07:15,203
In school, a teenage girl in nineteen

207
00:07:15,203 --> 00:07:17,243
sixty-five took English,

208
00:07:17,243 --> 00:07:19,454
history, math and science,

209
00:07:19,454 --> 00:07:22,599
along with home economics and typing.

210
00:07:22,779 --> 00:07:27,137
Guidance counselors often steered girls toward secretarial

211
00:07:27,137 --> 00:07:29,316
courses, nursing or teaching.

212
00:07:29,496 --> 00:07:32,985
Few girls were encouraged to become doctors,

213
00:07:32,985 --> 00:07:34,651
lawyers or engineers.

214
00:07:34,831 --> 00:07:36,363
Some girls listened.

215
00:07:36,543 --> 00:07:38,377
Others had other ideas.

216
00:07:38,727 --> 00:07:41,258
Sports options for girls were limited.

217
00:07:41,438 --> 00:07:44,089
Many schools had girls' gym class,

218
00:07:44,089 --> 00:07:47,441
in one-piece gym suits that everyone hated.

219
00:07:47,621 --> 00:07:49,737
There might be a few teams,

220
00:07:49,737 --> 00:07:52,244
like volleyball or field hockey,

221
00:07:52,244 --> 00:07:54,831
but rarely with real competition.

222
00:07:55,011 --> 00:07:57,461
Title Nine, which would open the door

223
00:07:57,461 --> 00:07:58,586
to girls' sports,

224
00:07:58,586 --> 00:08:00,374
was still seven years away.

225
00:08:00,724 --> 00:08:03,801
So many girls joined other activities.

226
00:08:03,981 --> 00:08:06,510
Cheerleading, which was one of the most

227
00:08:06,510 --> 00:08:08,521
popular things a girl could do.

228
00:08:08,701 --> 00:08:09,631
The pep club,

229
00:08:09,631 --> 00:08:10,634
the glee club,

230
00:08:10,634 --> 00:08:13,426
the student newspaper and the yearbook.

231
00:08:13,606 --> 00:08:15,870
Future Homemakers of America.

232
00:08:16,050 --> 00:08:18,188
And everyone wanted to be on the

233
00:08:18,188 --> 00:08:19,324
homecoming court.

234
00:08:19,504 --> 00:08:22,072
Being crowned homecoming queen was one of

235
00:08:22,072 --> 00:08:24,264
the biggest honors in a small town.

236
00:08:24,613 --> 00:08:26,090
And for many girls,

237
00:08:26,090 --> 00:08:28,655
the space program was a source of

238
00:08:28,655 --> 00:08:29,199
wonder.

239
00:08:29,379 --> 00:08:31,336
In nineteen sixty-three,

240
00:08:31,336 --> 00:08:34,188
the Soviet Union had sent the first

241
00:08:34,188 --> 00:08:35,574
woman into space,

242
00:08:35,574 --> 00:08:37,286
Valentina Tereshkova.

243
00:08:37,466 --> 00:08:40,420
American girls who dreamed of being astronauts

244
00:08:40,420 --> 00:08:42,539
were told that only men could go.

245
00:08:42,719 --> 00:08:45,727
It would take until nineteen eighty-three for

246
00:08:45,727 --> 00:08:48,334
Sally Ride to become the first American

247
00:08:48,334 --> 00:08:49,337
woman in space.

248
00:08:49,517 --> 00:08:51,892
Some of the girls of sixty-five cheered

249
00:08:51,892 --> 00:08:53,232
her on as grown women.

250
00:08:53,582 --> 00:08:56,375
Dating in nineteen sixty-five had a lot

251
00:08:56,375 --> 00:08:57,019
of rules.

252
00:08:57,199 --> 00:08:59,081
The boy asked the girl,

253
00:08:59,081 --> 00:09:00,472
usually by phone,

254
00:09:00,472 --> 00:09:02,273
a few days in advance.

255
00:09:02,453 --> 00:09:04,173
He came to the door and met

256
00:09:04,173 --> 00:09:04,937
her parents.

257
00:09:05,117 --> 00:09:07,376
Her father asked where they were going

258
00:09:07,376 --> 00:09:09,041
and what time she'd be home.

259
00:09:09,221 --> 00:09:11,096
And a girl who called a boy,

260
00:09:11,096 --> 00:09:13,373
instead of waiting for him to call

261
00:09:13,373 --> 00:09:15,584
her, risked being called forward.

262
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:18,297
A typical date was a movie,

263
00:09:18,297 --> 00:09:19,609
a school dance,

264
00:09:19,609 --> 00:09:22,235
a football game or a drive-in,

265
00:09:22,235 --> 00:09:25,036
followed by a burger and a Coke.

266
00:09:25,216 --> 00:09:26,897
In nineteen sixty-five,

267
00:09:26,897 --> 00:09:29,455
the movies were The Sound of Music,

268
00:09:29,455 --> 00:09:32,233
Doctor Zhivago and the James Bond film

269
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:33,110
Thunderball.

270
00:09:33,290 --> 00:09:35,068
And if a couple got serious,

271
00:09:35,068 --> 00:09:36,147
they went steady.

272
00:09:36,327 --> 00:09:38,312
She wore his class ring on a

273
00:09:38,312 --> 00:09:40,297
chain, or his letter jacket.

274
00:09:40,647 --> 00:09:41,620
For many girls,

275
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:43,500
the big night of the year was

276
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:44,084
the prom.

277
00:09:44,264 --> 00:09:45,599
Weeks of planning.

278
00:09:45,779 --> 00:09:46,897
A long gown,

279
00:09:46,897 --> 00:09:49,413
often homemade or borrowed.

280
00:09:49,593 --> 00:09:51,149
Long white gloves.

281
00:09:51,329 --> 00:09:53,140
A corsage from her date.

282
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:55,672
A professional photo in front of a

283
00:09:55,672 --> 00:09:56,710
paper backdrop.

284
00:09:56,890 --> 00:09:58,664
And a dance in the gym,

285
00:09:58,664 --> 00:10:01,594
decorated with crepe paper and a theme

286
00:10:01,594 --> 00:10:04,525
like Moonlight in Paris or Stairway to

287
00:10:04,525 --> 00:10:05,296
the Stars.

288
00:10:05,645 --> 00:10:07,859
Her parents watched all of this with

289
00:10:07,859 --> 00:10:09,396
a mix of pride and worry.

290
00:10:09,576 --> 00:10:12,499
Her mother remembered being a teenager during

291
00:10:12,499 --> 00:10:14,968
the war, and her father remembered the

292
00:10:14,968 --> 00:10:15,682
Depression.

293
00:10:15,862 --> 00:10:17,886
They had worked hard to give her

294
00:10:17,886 --> 00:10:18,772
a better life,

295
00:10:18,772 --> 00:10:19,910
with her own room,

296
00:10:19,910 --> 00:10:21,997
her own record player and her own

297
00:10:21,997 --> 00:10:22,503
clothes.

298
00:10:22,683 --> 00:10:24,934
But they worried about the new music,

299
00:10:24,934 --> 00:10:27,490
the short skirts and the long-haired boys.

300
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:30,131
Every generation of parents does.

301
00:10:30,481 --> 00:10:34,318
A teenage girl in nineteen sixty-five lived

302
00:10:34,318 --> 00:10:35,567
by many rules.

303
00:10:35,747 --> 00:10:37,386
Be home by curfew,

304
00:10:37,386 --> 00:10:39,206
often ten or eleven.

305
00:10:39,386 --> 00:10:41,047
Don't wear too much makeup.

306
00:10:41,227 --> 00:10:42,846
Don't go to a boy's house when

307
00:10:42,846 --> 00:10:44,141
his parents aren't home.

308
00:10:44,321 --> 00:10:45,837
Don't ride in a car with a

309
00:10:45,837 --> 00:10:46,945
boy you don't know.

310
00:10:47,125 --> 00:10:48,061
And above all,

311
00:10:48,061 --> 00:10:49,667
protect your reputation.

312
00:10:49,847 --> 00:10:51,235
In a small town,

313
00:10:51,235 --> 00:10:54,445
gossip could follow a girl for years.

314
00:10:54,795 --> 00:10:56,722
The expectation was clear.

315
00:10:56,902 --> 00:10:58,348
Finish high school,

316
00:10:58,348 --> 00:11:01,547
maybe go to college or secretarial school,

317
00:11:01,547 --> 00:11:02,765
and get married.

318
00:11:02,945 --> 00:11:04,515
In nineteen sixty-five,

319
00:11:04,515 --> 00:11:07,382
the typical American woman married for the

320
00:11:07,382 --> 00:11:09,226
first time at about twenty.

321
00:11:09,406 --> 00:11:11,929
Many girls were engaged by the time

322
00:11:11,929 --> 00:11:13,876
they graduated high school.

323
00:11:14,056 --> 00:11:16,624
And many brides were pregnant with their

324
00:11:16,624 --> 00:11:18,293
first child within a year.

325
00:11:18,643 --> 00:11:20,536
But things were starting to change.

326
00:11:20,716 --> 00:11:22,245
In nineteen sixty-three,

327
00:11:22,245 --> 00:11:25,177
Betty Friedan's book The Feminine Mystique had

328
00:11:25,177 --> 00:11:27,789
asked whether women should want more than

329
00:11:27,789 --> 00:11:29,191
a house and a husband.

330
00:11:29,371 --> 00:11:30,642
Many mothers read it,

331
00:11:30,642 --> 00:11:32,882
and some of them told their daughters

332
00:11:32,882 --> 00:11:34,456
to get an education first.

333
00:11:34,636 --> 00:11:37,283
The birth control pill had been approved

334
00:11:37,283 --> 00:11:38,474
in nineteen sixty,

335
00:11:38,474 --> 00:11:41,451
and it would change women's lives enormously.

336
00:11:41,801 --> 00:11:44,729
The Civil Rights Act of nineteen sixty-four

337
00:11:44,729 --> 00:11:47,316
had made it illegal for most employers

338
00:11:47,316 --> 00:11:49,290
to discriminate based on sex.

339
00:11:49,470 --> 00:11:51,240
In nineteen sixty-six,

340
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:55,101
the National Organization for Women was founded.

341
00:11:55,281 --> 00:11:58,133
College enrollment for women was rising every

342
00:11:58,133 --> 00:11:58,450
year.

343
00:11:58,630 --> 00:12:01,525
The teenage girls of nineteen sixty-five would

344
00:12:01,525 --> 00:12:04,105
graduate into a very different world than

345
00:12:04,105 --> 00:12:06,119
the one their mothers had known.

346
00:12:06,468 --> 00:12:08,187
And many of them would change it.

347
00:12:08,367 --> 00:12:10,609
They became the first women in their

348
00:12:10,609 --> 00:12:12,291
families to finish college.

349
00:12:12,471 --> 00:12:15,357
They became teachers and nurses,

350
00:12:15,357 --> 00:12:16,889
but also doctors,

351
00:12:16,889 --> 00:12:19,775
lawyers, scientists, executives,

352
00:12:19,775 --> 00:12:21,759
judges and astronauts.

353
00:12:21,939 --> 00:12:24,888
Many raised families and had careers.

354
00:12:25,068 --> 00:12:27,349
They did things their mothers had only

355
00:12:27,349 --> 00:12:27,889
imagined.

356
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:30,227
If you were a teenage girl in

357
00:12:30,227 --> 00:12:33,654
nineteen sixty-five, you're in your late seventies

358
00:12:33,654 --> 00:12:33,928
now.

359
00:12:34,108 --> 00:12:36,686
Maybe you still have a forty-five of

360
00:12:36,686 --> 00:12:37,760
a Beatles song,

361
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:39,263
a faded prom picture,

362
00:12:39,263 --> 00:12:41,411
or a diary with a broken lock.

363
00:12:41,591 --> 00:12:44,316
Maybe you remember exactly where you were

364
00:12:44,316 --> 00:12:46,974
when you first heard Satisfaction on the

365
00:12:46,974 --> 00:12:47,373
radio.

366
00:12:47,722 --> 00:12:49,593
It was a time of rules and

367
00:12:49,593 --> 00:12:52,544
possibilities, of rollers and miniskirts,

368
00:12:52,544 --> 00:12:54,630
of the old world and the new.

369
00:12:54,810 --> 00:12:56,974
You lived right on the line between

370
00:12:56,974 --> 00:12:57,283
them.

371
00:12:57,463 --> 00:12:58,367
And in many ways,

372
00:12:58,367 --> 00:13:00,601
you were the generation that walked across

373
00:13:00,601 --> 00:13:00,761
it.

374
00:13:01,110 --> 00:13:03,015
So tell us in the comments.

375
00:13:03,195 --> 00:13:05,749
How old were you in nineteen sixty-five?

376
00:13:05,929 --> 00:13:07,440
What was your favorite song,

377
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:09,923
your favorite outfit and your favorite Beatle?

378
00:13:10,103 --> 00:13:11,886
What rule did your parents have that

379
00:13:11,886 --> 00:13:12,877
you'll never forget?

380
00:13:13,057 --> 00:13:14,509
We read every comment.

381
00:13:14,689 --> 00:13:17,068
Next time, we're looking at what Sunday

382
00:13:17,068 --> 00:13:19,936
really looked like in nineteen fifties America.

383
00:13:20,116 --> 00:13:21,835
Thank you for spending this time with

384
00:13:21,835 --> 00:13:21,974
me.
